WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.153
Jamming or strong interference can reduce signal quality or prevent a receiver from maintaining satellite lock.

00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:18.171
Possible effects include fewer usable satellites, poor accuracy estimates, navigation warnings, delayed position updates, or loss of position-dependent flight modes.

00:00:18.171 --> 00:00:26.324
The aircraft may drift or hold position less reliably when it transitions away from satellite-assisted stabilization.

00:00:26.324 --> 00:00:32.148
Automated missions can pause, abort, or behave according to a failsafe.

00:00:32.148 --> 00:00:37.040
The exact response depends on the aircraft and configuration.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.398
Unexpected return behavior or a navigation-mode change deserves immediate attention.

00:00:42.398 --> 00:00:47.290
Read warnings, maintain visual awareness, and reduce mission complexity.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:53.114
Do not dismiss an alert because the map still looks plausible.

00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:56.609
Similar symptoms can have non-malicious causes.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:06.160
Buildings, terrain, foliage, antenna orientation, reflected signals, weather-related obstruction, power infrastructure, damaged equipment, or software faults can degrade reception.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.916
Localized radio noise from faulty or incompatible equipment can also resemble intentional interference.

00:01:12.916 --> 00:01:22.001
Note whether the problem follows one aircraft, one controller, one location, or multiple independent systems before drawing conclusions.

00:01:22.001 --> 00:01:34.348
Look for a pattern across multiple indicators: satellite count or status, quality estimates, map behavior, visual position, flight-mode changes, compass information, and the physical environment.

00:01:34.348 --> 00:01:38.308
A single changing number remains weak evidence.

00:01:38.308 --> 00:01:41.336
The safety response is conservative.

00:01:41.336 --> 00:01:53.683
Stop automated collection, keep the aircraft in sight, avoid flying farther, and return or land using trained procedures before battery, signal, or orientation margins narrow.

00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:58.575
Diagnose cause after the aircraft and people are safe.
